1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
German public procurement publishes more notices than any contractor can read.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Finding them is easy. Deciding which ones to bid on is the expensive part,
and it needs two kinds of knowledge that never sit in the same place.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This site is called Ausschreibungsagenten.de — translated: Tender Agents.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:30,000
It registers 11 tools, to be exact: 11 WebMCP tools for search, details,
source freshness, and a shared Go/No-Go board.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Look for facade tenders in Germany, put the two with the longest deadlines
on the board, and explain them.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Please watch the page, not the chat.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The search runs. The result list changes. Two tenders go on the board,
each with its reasons.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:06,000
CPV division, place of performance, days to deadline, and contract value
against the EU threshold for construction.

9
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Whether the award is on price alone and how many lots. Those reasons come
from fields the result card never shows.

10
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Now I disagree. The one on the left is out of range because its place of
performance is not specified.

11
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So, what is on the board now?

12
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
It reads back a decision it did not make.

13
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:46,000
That is the whole point: one artifact, two parties,
and the human keeps the last word.

14
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Three things this does not do. No tool submits a form. The pilot request
emails a real person, so an agent may fill it in,

15
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
but sending stays a human click. Tools returning notice text are marked as
untrusted content. That text comes from 17 third-party portals.

16
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:15,000
And no tool returns a recommendation — only arguments with a sign and a count.

17
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Ausschreibungsagenten, or Tender Agents, is built on a live procurement
platform.

18
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The code is on GitHub under an MIT license: 11 tools, 43 tests.
The link to the original notice always prevails over anything we show.
